56. The Indian Industrial Sector: Policy, Sickness and Regional Imbalance
Paper: Industrial Economics (ECNDSE303) | Unit: VI | Topic: Evolution of Indian industries, industrial policy, globalisation, small-scale industries, industrial sickness, mergers and acquisitions evidence, locational trends, regional imbalance, patent policy
Introduction
Indian industry has moved from colonial-era stagnation through state-led import substitution to a liberalised, globalised structure.
Evolution
• Pre-1947: textiles (Bombay, Ahmedabad), jute, steel (TISCO 1907); limited base, deindustrialisation of handicrafts.
• 1950-65: planned industrialisation, with the Second Plan (Mahalanobis model) emphasising heavy industry and public sector.
• 1965-80: controls, licensing, shortages, slow growth.
• 1980s: partial liberalisation.
• Post-1991: liberalisation; services growth; Make in India (2014), PLI schemes (2020) and semiconductor and electronics push.
Industrial policy
• IPR 1948: mixed economy, role of state and private sector.
• IPR 1956: the "economic constitution"; Schedule A (state monopoly), B (progressive state ownership), C (private). Industries (Development and Regulation) Act 1951 gave licensing control.
• 1969-77: MRTP Act, FERA, licensing, nationalisation, reservation for small scale.
• 1991 New Industrial Policy: abolition of licensing (except a few), liberalised FDI, MRTP limits removed, public sector reserved list cut, disinvestment, reduced tariffs. MRTP replaced by Competition Act 2002.
• Recent: National Manufacturing Policy, Make in India, PLI, Atmanirbhar Bharat, Industrial corridors.
Globalisation
Lowered barriers, raised FDI, exposed firms to competition, boosted software, pharmaceuticals, autos; but hurt some small firms and raised import dependence (electronics, chemicals).
Small-scale industries (MSMEs)
Important for employment (over 11 crore jobs), exports (about 45 per cent), and dispersal. MSMED Act 2006 defines by investment and turnover (revised 2020). Problems: credit, technology, marketing, delayed payments, competition from big firms. Support: Mudra, credit guarantee, cluster development.
Industrial sickness
A unit is sick (SICA, 1985) if accumulated losses equal or exceed net worth. Causes: internal (poor management, wrong location, labour issues, technological obsolescence) and external (demand fall, input shortages, policy changes, delayed payment). Remedies: BIFR (abolished), now Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code 2016, NCLT, rehabilitation and revival schemes.
Mergers and acquisitions and diversification: evidence
Since the 1990s, M&As have grown (Tata-Corus, Vodafone-Hutch, Bank mergers 2019-20). Studies (Beena, Kumar) find mixed results: modest improvement in profitability for some, limited gains for most; diversified business groups, which use internal capital markets, perform variably after liberalisation.
Locational trends and regional imbalance
Industry is concentrated in Maharashtra, Gujarat, Tamil Nadu, Karnataka and Haryana, while the east and north-east lag. Causes: infrastructure, agglomeration, freight equalisation policy (which removed the advantage of mineral-rich states), post-1991 FDI flowing to developed states. Remedies: industrial corridors (DMIC), SEZs, backward area incentives (NE industrial policy, special category status), cluster development.
Patent policy
Patents Act 1970 (process patents, short duration, food and drug exceptions) supported generic drug industry. Amended in 1999, 2002, and 2005 to comply with TRIPS (product patents from 2005). Section 3(d) restricts evergreening (Novartis v. Union of India, 2013). Compulsory licensing (Natco-Bayer, 2012). Balance between innovation and access.
Conclusion
India's industrial sector has shifted toward markets and global integration, but challenges remain: jobs, manufacturing share, MSME constraints, and regional disparities.